Party offices become storerooms, transport desks and temporary host registries.
The supplies move quickly because organisers ignore institutional boundaries
and solve the problem in front of them.

They also begin to burn out. Families make private commitments no budget has
replaced, and the government learns that applause for volunteers is cheaper
than designing a reception system.

