= September 2022 — Who absorbs the shock?

Russian supply cuts and replacement purchases move through state utilities into
the bills of households, hospitals, schools and small firms. PiS promises caps,
KO attacks the government's energy record and Konfederacja treats every levy
and climate rule as part of the same failure.

Lewica must choose who receives a universal guarantee, who pays for it and
whether this winter accelerates public transition or suspends it. Every answer
protects somebody first and leaves somebody else carrying the uncertainty.

- @sep22_cap
- @sep22_public
- @sep22_target
- @sep22_deregulate

@sep22_cap
title: Cap household prices and levy extraordinary profits
subtitle: Give every household a clear ceiling and make energy windfalls finance it.
on-arrival: household_policy_pressure += 6; labor_credibility += 4; budget_promises += 2; left_economic_position -= 4; pis_economic_position -= 2; last_policy_stage = "Proposal"; last_policy_authority = "Parliamentary opposition"; last_policy_implementation = 0; news_headline = "A windfall-funded cap becomes the social opposition line"

Tenant groups, unions and pensioners receive a number they can plan around. The
windfall levy lets Lewica say who should finance the guarantee rather than
placing its entire cost in an unspecified future budget.

Large energy firms dispute what counts as an extraordinary profit, and the
promise is expensive if the levy falls short. PiS begins borrowing the cap
while resisting our account of who should pay.

- @poland_hub: Continue


@sep22_public
title: Pair short relief with public-energy investment
subtitle: Cost: 2 resources.
